When small groups of organisms (with small populations) become separated from their original main population (for example, if they migrate along a mountain range or river, or move to a new island where they cannot easily return), they often find themselves in environments very different from those they previously experienced.
This new environment presents both new resources and different competitors, so new populations must possess different traits or adaptability in order to become stronger competitors than what they previously faced.
The original population has not changed at all; they still require adaptation as before.
As time passed, as the new population gradually adapted to their new environment, they began to look less and less like the original population.
Eventually, after several thousand or even millions of years, the populations will become so different that they can no longer be considered the same species.
We call this process speciation, which refers to the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a crucial part of evolution.
